22:20:05 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your observation is actually spot on! You aren't wrong—the water level *barely* changed. The reason it didn't evaporate completely isn't because evaporation stopped, but because **the conditions on your nightstand are almost perfectly designed to prevent fast evaporation.** If you want to see evaporation happen quickly at room temperature, you need three things: **Surface Area, Airflow, and Dry Air.** Your glass lacks all three. Here is why your glass of water is "stuck" in a state of suspended evaporation: ### 1. The "Surface Area" Bottleneck Evaporation only happens at the surface. Think of the surface of the water like a doorway. * **A glass:** Has a very small "doorway" (the opening of the glass). Only a few molecules can exit at a time. * **A puddle or a wide pan:** Has a massive "doorway." Thousands of molecules can exit at once. Because your glass has such a small opening, the amount of water escaping is tiny—so tiny that you won't notice the water level dropping for weeks. ### 2. The "Invisible Lid" (Humidity) This is the most important factor. When water molecules evaporate, they don't just vanish into space; they hang out in the air immediately above the water. * If the air in your room is still (no breeze), the air right above the water in your glass becomes "saturated" with water vapor. * Imagine a crowded room with only one exit. If the hallway outside is already packed with people, nobody else can leave the room. * Because the air above your glass is "full" of water vapor, the water molecules have nowhere to go. They end up bouncing back into the water. This is called **equilibrium**. The air is so humid near the surface of the water that it essentially puts an "invisible lid" on your glass. ### 3. Lack of Airflow If you put that same glass of water in front of a fan, it would evaporate much faster. * A fan sweeps away the "saturated" air (the humid air) and replaces it with fresh, dry air. * On your nightstand, the air is likely very still. Without a breeze to carry the vapor away, the air right above the glass stays humid, keeping the evaporation rate extremely low. ### The "Proof" Experiment If you want to prove that evaporation is happening at room temperature, try this: 1. Take two identical glasses. 2. Fill one halfway with water. 3. Pour the water from the second glass into a **wide, shallow dinner plate** (or a frying pan). 4. Leave them both on the counter for 48 hours. You will find that the water in the plate is almost gone, while the water in the glass is still mostly there. **The conclusion:** You are right that it isn't evaporating *fast*, but it **is** evaporating. It is just being held back by the small opening of the glass and the stagnant, humid air sitting right on top of it. If you left that glass for a month or two, you *would* eventually see the water level drop significantly!
