22:21:36 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You are absolutely correct. If evaporation were a magical force that just made water disappear regardless of the environment, your towel would dry out instantly. The fact that your towel stays damp is actually the **perfect proof** that evaporation is not a magic trick—it is a physical process that depends entirely on the **conditions of the air around the water.** Your bathroom is essentially the "worst-case scenario" for evaporation. Here is why that towel is staying wet, and why it actually confirms the science: ### 1. The "Full Room" Problem (Saturation) Imagine a crowded elevator. If the elevator is packed with people, nobody else can get in, right? The air in your bathroom acts the same way. When you take a shower or run the sink, you fill the air in that room with water vapor. Once the air is "full" (scientists call this **100% relative humidity** or **saturation**), it physically cannot hold any more water. When the air is saturated, the water molecules in your towel have nowhere to go. They try to evaporate, but they immediately hit air that is already packed with other water molecules. They end up bouncing right back into the fabric of the towel. **This is why the towel doesn't dry.** ### 2. The "Equilibrium" Trap This is the key to your observation. Evaporation is a two-way street: * Water leaves the towel (evaporation). * Water returns to the towel from the air (condensation). In a dry room with good airflow, the "leaving" wins, and the towel dries. In a small, humid bathroom, the "returning" matches the "leaving." The towel is in a state of **equilibrium**. It is technically still evaporating, but it is also absorbing moisture from the humid air at the exact same rate. ### 3. Why this proves evaporation is real If you want to prove that it’s the **bathroom conditions** stopping the evaporation, and not the water itself, try this: Take that same damp towel out of the bathroom and hang it on a clothesline outside on a breezy da